{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>ויהי.</b> The word יהי was fitting to be like ירב המשא עליו (2 Chronicles 24:27), and because the heh is from the throat, they began with the word יהי; the first yod is in kamatz katan, יֶהִי, and the last yod was fitting to be with a resting shva na, like the bet in ירב. And it was fitting for it to be יהי, but it is heavy on the tongue, therefore they concealed it and punctuated the heh with chirik; therefore לו יְהִי (Genesis 30:34), כי הוא אמר וַיֶהִי (Psalms 33:9). And because they speak with this word frequently always, they returned the accent to the end (milra), and moved the yod with shva, יהי כן ד' (below 10:10). Therefore there is no word in the binyan kal (pa'al) pattern where the servant letter has a shva—except this formation and the formation of ויחי יעקב (Genesis 47:28). And this is because the two root letters of these two formations, ויהי and ויחי, are from the weak letters, namely yod and heh of היה, חיה, and because these two letters are among the weak letters, sometimes they do not appear at the end of the word, so the servant letter, which is the yod, comes upon them with shva—and not so in all other formations in the binyan kal (pa'al)." "<b>יוצאי ירך.</b> A hint to the limb close to him that begets, like להסך את רגליו (1 Samuel 24:4). For they were sixty-nine, but Jacob is counted first, therefore he wrote at the beginning את יעקב, as we said above. And in the first section, ויגש אליו, it is written יעקב ובניו (Genesis 46:8). And behold, with Jacob were all the souls of his sons and daughters, thirty-three (ibid. v. 15). And the proof: בשבעים נפש ירדו אבותיך מצרימה (Deuteronomy 10:22), for one soul was Jacob's, and Joseph who was in Egypt is in the count, he and his two sons."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Resolved ד' in יהי כן ד' as HaShem per standard reverent abbreviation; the phrase anticipates Exodus 10:10."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:44.499975973Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}